The '''Treaty of Tamsui''' of 1618 was an agreement between the [[Sinchew Colony]] and [[Hachinawa]] dividing between them the lands each had claimed in the country to the south and south-east of the empire. It concluded the [[Third Mahik War]] and established the boundary shown on maps of the period as the frontier of 1618.

The treaty was made between two parties and not between two powers: the colony was a possession of [[Zong]] and the empire was an independent state, and the agreement was negotiated at Tamsui between the colonial administration and the Hachina court without the participation of the Zong government. It is one of several instruments of the period by which the colony conducted its own foreign relations.

== Background ==

The area to the south and south-east of Hachinawa was held by [[Timara]] chiefdoms rather than by a single polity, and both the colony and the empire had claims upon it. The colony's claim followed from its settlement of the northern coast; the empire's followed from its long frontier with the Timara country and from the tributary relationships it maintained across it.

The two conferred in 1614 and agreed to divide the territory between them. The campaign that followed ended in their favour in 1618, and the treaty was concluded in the same year.

== Terms ==

The agreement divided the claimed lands on a boundary drawn between the colony and the empire and running from the northern coast to the southern frontier. Its provisions were of three kinds.

The division of the conquered country. The Timara country was divided between the two parties. The colony took the portion adjoining its own territory and the empire took the remainder, which adjoined its frontier along its whole length.

The boundary. The frontier between the colony and the empire was fixed along the line established by the agreement. No provision was made for its revision, and no commission of delimitation was established to mark it on the ground.

The Timara population. The treaty contains no provision concerning the Timara themselves, who were not a party to it and whose chiefdoms had been defeated in the campaign. Their position was determined by the practice of the two victors rather than by the agreement, and it differed on either side of the line: in the colony they entered the class recorded in the returns as free natives, and in the empire they were treated as a subject people of the frontier.

== Frontier of 1618 ==

Under the boundary established by the treaty, Hachinawa held about 1,581,000 km² and the Sinchew Colony about 600,000 km². The division placed the greater part of the territory east and south of the colony's own lands, and it made the empire for a period the larger of the two polities on the western coast.

The line remained the frontier between the two until the colonial period ended. The greater portion of the territory assigned to Hachinawa in 1618 forms part of the modern territory of [[Sinchew]], which extends over both the colony's portion and the empire's.

== Aftermath ==

Hachinawa's share of the conquest was the largest single extension of its territory, and it was obtained without purchase by an empire that had been selling frontier land to the colony for forty years.

For the colony the treaty secured its southern frontier and added territory to it, and it did not relieve the pressure that had produced the agreement. The land acquired in 1618 was added to a colony that could not feed the population it already held, and the settlement of the Timara country was carried out on the same terms as the settlement of the lands bought from Hachinawa: on ground the administration did not want, by a population excluded from office and from grants.

